## The n8n stack on this machine

- n8n is pinned to 1.54.0, not the 2.41.6 that PLAN.md originally called
  for. Before moving to 2.x, re-check DB_TYPE, the DB_POSTGRESDB_* prefix
  and the SSL behaviour, then re-run ./test-setup.sh from a clean volume.
- DB_POSTGRESDB_SSL_ENABLED must stay unset on 1.54.0: the option has an
  untyped (Object) design type, so the string "false" stays truthy.
- The stack runs from the repository root, so the compose project is "n8n"
  and the volumes are n8n_postgres-data and n8n_n8n-data. A stack started
  from a worktree used a different project name and its own volumes.
- The n8n owner account and the Ollama credential live in the database, not
  in the repository. n8n stores the owner password only as a bcrypt hash, so
  it cannot be read back out of the database. A copy of the generated
  password is kept outside the repository at
  ~/.n8n-lab/owner-credentials.txt (mode 600). If that file is lost too,
  reset the password with `docker exec n8n n8n user-management:reset`, which
  clears the instance back to the first-run owner setup.

## Ollama on this machine

- Ollama 0.35.1 runs natively as a Homebrew service, using a custom plist
  at config/ollama/sh.brew.ollama.plist:
      brew services start ollama --file=config/ollama/sh.brew.ollama.plist
  The formula's own plist does not set the two variables below, and
  "brew services start ollama" overwrites a custom plist, so always pass
  --file.
- OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS=1 is required. The 24B variant (15 GB) and the
  8B variant (6 GB) cannot both be resident on this 32 GB machine; Metal
  fails with "Insufficient Memory" and the model server is killed.
- OLLAMA_NO_CLOUD=1 keeps prompts off Ollama's cloud services.
- The lab model variants are lab-llama3.1-8b and lab-mistral-small3.2-24b,
  both at a 16,384 token context, built from the Modelfiles in models/.
- Docker Desktop's memory limit is NOT in effect: settings.json says
  memoryMiB 8092, but the running VM reports 15.6 GB. It was reset by a
  Docker Desktop update. Re-applying it is a manual step.

## Driving n8n through its REST API

- The n8n image has no psql client and the editor UI is not scriptable, so
  use the REST API with a session cookie.
- Create the owner: POST /rest/owner/setup with email, firstName, lastName
  and password. The password policy is 8-64 characters, at least one digit
  and at least one uppercase letter.
- Log in: POST /rest/login with "email" and "password" (not
  emailOrLdapLoginId).
- Create a credential: POST /rest/credentials with name, type and data. Test
  it with POST /rest/credentials/test and the same payload.
- Run a workflow: POST /rest/workflows/<id>/run needs the whole workflow in
  the body as workflowData, not just an id.
- Execution run data is stored flattened, with nested values as stringified
  indices. scripts/show_execution.py resolves it.
